            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal />
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings />
                <HotWaterSystem />
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with a combination of 2 systems:



1. A 95% AFUE "combi" gas boiler that is providing in-floor heat to the basement of the house, as well as to the slab of the solarium (part of the indoor heated living space we model) and the adjoined garage (not part of the indoor heated living space). This combi boiler also provides your domestic hot water (on-demand, with a 16 USG internal buffer tank) at a calculated Energy Factor of 0.86.



2. A 96% AFUE gas furnace that heats the upper floor of the house, however (based on the design of the ductwork &amp; open concept of your house &amp; solarium) has the ability to heat the entire house. The furnace plenum has a central air conditioning (CAC) evaporator coil installed, however there is no outside condenser unit.



To manage heating level comfort of your entire living space (house &amp; solarium), you estimate the above 2 systems contribute equally (50/50) to the heating of your house ........... and I have modeled them this way in my analyses. In this analyses, I have not modeled any CAC energy load for your house, as a condenser unit does not exist.



At current, you are trying to decide if you will just install an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified CAC condenser (for cooling load only), or if you may decide to install a cold climate air source heat pump (ccASHP) to provide both heating &amp; cooling to your house. This decision will have a few factors to consider:

   - The heating COP &amp; cooling SEER that can be obtained with a ccASHP

   - The fuel cost differential between piped gas (for your 95% &amp; 96% AFUE units) and electricity for the ccASHP

   - The GHG offset as you convert heating load from fossil fuel to clean hydro-electricity

   - The dependability of a ccASHP in your weather region

   - Any rebates/incentives available 



I have modeled the option for you to install a ccASHP that meets the requirements of Natural Resource Canada's Greener Homes Grant initiative. At this time, I have just used the default sizing inputs that NRCan includes in the software that we use to energy evaluate housing:

   - 7 kW

   - HSPF (Region V) of 5.9

   - I have chosen "Heating Only" mode (to match your current house setup)

        ** You will eventually have a full CAC system in your house, so choosing a ccASHP with a high SEER for cooling

            is also important when deciding what equipment you may purchase. 



Savings: By installing a ccASHP that meets the minimum requirements outlined above, your annual energy use is estimated to decrease by 8.6 GJ, however annual energy costs (gas savings less cost of additional electricity) are expected to increase by about $ 292. A qualified ccASHP can be eligible for up $5,000 from the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative.



Heat Pumps- Air source heat pump- ASHP and ccASHP



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $2,500 is available for smaller Air Source Heat Pumps (ASHP) or cold climate Air Source Heat Pumps (ccASHP). $4,000 is available for larger ASHPs and $5,000 for large ccASHPs, depending upon the type installed. Speak with an HVAC professional for recommendations on the type of heat pump that is best suited for your home, for example, an air-source heat pump, or if you live in a colder climate, a cold-climate heat pump.



Depending upon the region in which you live and how you heat your home today, the installation of a heat pump in your home may result in higher utility costs. Consult with an HVAC professional in this field to determine if this type of system is practical and the right choice for your circumstances.



To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.



The design heating load of your home is: 7.62 kW. Your new heat pump must be sized to supply heat to your entire home. Ask the professional to size your heat pump according to the heating load of your entire home as indicated above.



For more information: Natural Resources Canada has developed a package of materials related to air source heat pump sizing and selection, intended for use by mechanical system designers and renovation contractors. https://www.nrcan.gc.ca/maps-tools-and-publications/tools/modelling-tools/toolkit-for-air-source-heat-pump-sizing-and-selection/23558</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Renewable Energy Systems



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1,300 per KW is available for installing a solar photovoltaic system technology that converts sunlight directly into electricity. If you choose to install a photovoltaic (PV) system, it is strongly recommended that a full assessment by a professional solar photovoltaic installer, including the measuring of solar irradiance (how much sunlight falls on the roof) is undertaken to provide detailed information on considerations for your home and specific installation recommendations, including the size and related energy production of the system. The rated PV panel must have peak power capacity higher than or equal to 1.0 kW. The system must be composed of photovoltaic (PV) panel and inverter certified to CSA Standards. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.



Batteries Connected to Photovoltaic Systems to Provide Standby Power for Home



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1300 is available for the installation of battery storage and an inverter to connect to a photovoltaic system to provide standby power for the home for an existing PV system or a new install. This resiliency measure must be combined with an energy efficiency retrofit from the Canada Greener Homes Grant in order to be eligible. This grant may be combined with the new PV system installation grant for a maximum grant of $5000. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.



Savings: As a starting point for your further investigation, I modeled a 4 kW PV system, flush mounted on the rear south facing roof (11.5 degrees east of true south), directly connected to the grid, without a battery-based standby power system. This is estimated to produce about 4,331 kWh annually, reducing your electricity bill by $ 1,446 and qualify for the full $5,000 from the Canada Greener Homes program.</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.



Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.



ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION



For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.



Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:



????????? In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report

????????? If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.

????????? Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit

????????? All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional

????????? It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:

     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*

     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards

     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home

*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.

????????? All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.



ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES



You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.



Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs and tubes are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment that vents to the outside (for your house (the 240 CFM range hood &amp; the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 30.5 Pascals. As this number is greater than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment could be expected to draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current combi boiler and furnace are direct vent, and you indicated that you are not planning to install a wood or pellet stove. If you do install a wood or pellet stove, than please ensure a dedicated &amp; sealed outside combustion air supply pipe is provided directly to the wood or pellet stove.



During my assessment of your home, I noticed various combination smoke &amp; carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following ventilation:

        - A Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) unit - balanced

        - The kitchen range hood - exhaust only

        - 3 x bathrooms - exhaust through the HRV via boost switches

        - The clothes dryer - exhaust only



Your house is quite air tight and the kitchen range hood &amp; clothes dryer can create a high level of depressurization (meaning they are trying to suck out more air than can easily be replenished from within the house). All air tight houses will experience this with "exhaust only" appliances, and it's OK as long as there are no "chimney vented" heating appliances in the house - which your house does not.



One thing that you may notice is that your clothes dryer may take a little longer to dry clothes ...... as it is not really able to suck the full CFM through the chamber that many of the "timed" dryer settings were programmed based upon. If your dryer has a "sensor" setting on it, you may find using this setting provides better results.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Hay River



The following Dec. 1, 2021 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Hay River:

Electricity - $19.66 min, $0.33 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.36 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $1.14 per litre 

Propane - $0.86 per litre delivered, $3.31 per m3 piped to the house

Wood - $341.25 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.20 per bag, $268.12 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: ken.baigent@aea.nt.ca

Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!



